  "notes": "{儚|はかな}い expresses the transient, ephemeral nature of things, often with a sense of beauty or sadness.\n\nFORMS:\n- Attributive: {儚|はかな}い + noun\n- Predicate: {儚|はかな}い\n- Adverbial: {儚|はかな}く\n- Noun form: {儚|はかな}さ (transience)\n\nWRITING:\n- The kanji {儚|はかな} ({人|にんべん} + {夢|ゆめ}) literally suggests \"person + dream,\" evoking the dreamlike, insubstantial quality of fleeting things.\n- Often written in hiragana (はかない) in modern usage.\n\nCULTURAL NOTE:\n- Central to Japanese aesthetics ({物|もの}の{哀|あわ}れ)\n- Associated with cherry blossoms, fireflies, and other symbols of transience\n- Reflects Buddhist concepts of impermanence ({無常|むじょう})\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {儚|はかな}い{命|いのち} (fleeting life)\n- {儚|はかな}い{夢|ゆめ} (ephemeral dream)\n- {儚|はかな}い{美|うつく}しさ (transient beauty)\n- {儚|はかな}い{希望|きぼう} (fragile hope)\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {短|みじか}い (short - neutral, objective)\n- {束|つか}の{間|ま} (momentary - time-focused)\n- {脆|もろ}い (fragile - physical fragility)\n\nREGISTER: Literary/formal. Often used in poetry and emotional contexts.",
